  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">High blood pressure reading (R03.0)</content>. Ambulatory monitoring confirms hypertension in the stage 2 range (daytime average 146/94 mmHg), with no white-coat effect. Dr. Lindqvist-Owusu finds mild concentric left ventricular hypertrophy that fits hypertension added to training, with normal function; kidneys, urine albumin, glucose and thyroid are normal, and there is no sign of a secondary cause beyond alcohol. The high reading that brought him in is resolved into the diagnosis of hypertension. [S]<list><item>Start amlodipine 5 mg and valsartan 160 mg in one tablet each morning.</item><item>Potassium and creatinine at the four-week visit.</item><item>A talking home blood pressure monitor with a large display: two readings each morning, photographed and sent to the clinic by text message.</item><item>Lower-salt eating and fewer drinks.</item><item>No bouts until his blood pressure is controlled; then the commission physician&apos;s check.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Alcohol use disorder, moderate (F10.20)</content>. A good start on his own goal; weekends are the hard part. He wants to keep trying without medicine for now. [S]<list><item>Keep the tally card; plan alcohol-free weekend evenings with his trainer&apos;s sessions.</item><item>Naltrexone, acamprosate and topiramate explained again for if he wants help.</item><item>Safety plan reviewed.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Office blood pressure (seated, large cuff, average of two readings), weight and BMI, heart sounds</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>148/96 mmHg; weight 98.6 kg; no gallop</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Out-of-office blood pressure (ambulatory or home monitoring)</td><td>At diagnosis and after each treatment change</td><td>Completed today</td><td>24-hour ambulatory monitoring last week: daytime 146/94, nighttime 132/84 mmHg; confirms hypertension</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Potassium, creatinine and eGFR</td><td>Baseline; two to four weeks after starting an ARB; then every six months on an ARB</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Baseline potassium 4.1 mmol/L and creatinine 1.1 mg/dL (eGFR 89) at intake; recheck at four weeks after starting valsartan</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Lipids, glucose or A1c, urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio, TSH</td><td>At diagnosis, then yearly (TSH at diagnosis)</td><td>Up to date</td><td>At intake: LDL 122, HDL 58 mg/dL; A1c 5.4%; urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio 18 mg/g; TSH 1.6</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>12-lead ECG</td><td>Baseline at diagnosis</td><td>Up to date</td><td>At intake: sinus bradycardia 56; LVH voltage criteria without repolarization change</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Echocardiogram</td><td>At diagnosis for stage 2 hypertension or suspected LVH; repeat as indicated</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Mild concentric LVH (12 mm); ejection fraction 60 percent; normal diastolic function and aortic root</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Fundoscopic examination</td><td>Yearly</td><td>Due</td><td>At the preventive visit</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Cardiovascular risk estimate</td><td>Yearly</td><td>Due</td><td>At the preventive visit; the pooled cohort equations apply only from 40</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Antihypertensive tolerance (ankle edema, dizziness) and adherence by teach-back</td><td>Each follow-up and each dose change</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No antihypertensive until today; teach-back of the new tablet done with the picture card</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Fitness to compete (AHA/ACC athlete statement)</td><td>At diagnosis and before returning to competition</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Cardiology: no competition until controlled; then clearance with a repeat check</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Drinking (AUDIT-C, drinks per week, drinking days)</td><td>Every follow-up during treatment; full AUDIT at about four months</td><td>Up to date</td><td>AUDIT 16 at intake; about 18 drinks a week now by his tally; next AUDIT-C at four weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Liver tests (AST, ALT, GGT)</td><td>Baseline; about two months after starting naltrexone or cutting down; then as indicated</td><td>Up to date</td><td>At intake: AST 46, ALT 38, GGT 88 U/L; recheck in about two months</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide-risk review (structured questions) and safety plan</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No thoughts of suicide; safety plan card reviewed</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Health literacy: teach-back, picture card, read-aloud consent, voice reminders</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Plan explained in two key points; he repeated them back correctly; consent for the echocardiogram read aloud</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Confirm or rule out hypertension with out-of-office readings before any medicine. Outcome by 2026-06-23: Ambulatory monitoring confirmed stage 2 hypertension (daytime 146/94 mmHg) [S]</item><item>Blood pressure under 130/80 mmHg in the clinic and at home by four months. In progress; target 2026-10-20 [S]</item><item>Drink within the goal he set: no more than 14 drinks a week and no more than 4 on any day, and none in the two weeks before a fight. In progress; target 2026-10-20 [S]</item><item>Explain his medicines and blood pressure plan back in his own words. In progress; target 2026-07-21 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: 2025 AHA/ACC/AANP/AAPA/ABC/ACCP/ACPM/AGS/AMA/ASPC/NMA/PCNA/SGIM Guideline for the Prevention, Detection, Evaluation and Management of High Blood Pressure in Adults (2025); Hypertension in Adults: Screening (2021); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, hypertension and depression monographs (2026); Eligibility and Disqualification Recommendations for Competitive Athletes With Cardiovascular Abnormalities: Task Force 6: Hypertension (Black HR, et al.) (2015); The Healthcare Professional&apos;s Core Resource on Alcohol (2026); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); AHRQ Health Literacy Universal Precautions Toolkit, 3rd Edition (2024)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Four-week review with potassium, creatinine and AUDIT-C. Home readings each morning by photo message. Call 988 or the clinic at any time if thoughts of suicide arise. Next scheduled visit 2026-07-21: four-week review: blood pressure on treatment, potassium and creatinine, drinking, and medicine teach-back. [S]</paragraph></text>

